  {"page":152,"id":"u5-a9","label":"Unit 5 · Activity 9","flag":[7.2,84.2],"answer":"In these two excerpts, the difference lies in the figurative use of language. In the second excerpt, there were uses of onomatopoeia (Thunk, thunk) simile (soft pine needles covered the ground like a soft brown blanket; as anxious as a fish out of water), alliteration (twittered and tweeted), hyperbole (the sky-scraping branches of trees), and personification (made his heart dance). It creates images in the readers’ mind."},
  {"page":153,"id":"u5-a10-t0-1","label":"Unit 5 · Activity 10 · 1","flag":[7.2,57.2],"answer":"Using Alliteration: Tanysha sat up late in her bedroom trying to study. Tanysha sat up late in her bedroom silently and studiously trying to study."},
  {"page":153,"id":"u5-a10-t0-2","label":"Unit 5 · Activity 10 · 2","flag":[7.2,63.4],"answer":"Using Hyperbole: She was an all-A student… She was such a great student that anything less than a 100++ would send her sobbing from the room."},
  {"page":153,"id":"u5-a10-t0-3","label":"Unit 5 · Activity 10 · 3","flag":[7.2,69.4],"answer":"Using Simile: … and tomorrow’s math test was an important one. Tomorrow’s math test was as important to Tanysha as a candidate’s final election speech is in the presidential election."},
  {"page":153,"id":"u5-a10-t0-4","label":"Unit 5 · Activity 10 · 4","flag":[7.2,75.6],"answer":"Using Onomatopoeia: Lying back on her bed for a minute… With a squeak of bedsprings and the crunch of crumpled paper, Tanysha lay back on her bed for a minute."},
  {"page":153,"id":"u5-a10-t0-5","label":"Unit 5 · Activity 10 · 5","flag":[7.2,81.8],"answer":"Using Metaphor: … Tanysha started daydreaming about going to college and becoming a doctor one day. She started daydreaming about going to the ivory tower learning to become a disease fighter one day."},
  {"page":159,"id":"u6-a1-climax","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 1 · climax","flag":[42.3,31.5],"answer":"Coyote attacked what he thought was the dove, broke his teeth, and began to moan."},
  {"page":159,"id":"u6-a1-falling","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 1 · falling","flag":[58.6,36.8],"answer":"A crow passed by and called the moan a beautiful song."},
  {"page":159,"id":"u6-a1-rising","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 1 · rising","flag":[20.2,37],"answer":"Coyote insisted it was a song and tried to take it home, but tripped and forgot it repeatedly."},
  {"page":159,"id":"u6-a1-exposition","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 1 · exposition","flag":[14.4,44.3],"answer":"A dove hurt her hands; Coyote mistook her moaning for a song."},
  {"page":159,"id":"u6-a1-resolution","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 1 · resolution","flag":[69.1,44.5],"answer":"Coyote said the crows could not distinguish singing from crying—exactly his earlier mistake."},
  {"page":161,"id":"u6-a2-guardian-rising","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · guardian-rising","flag":[7.2,52.5],"answer":"Sam caught the lodger stealing the money, recovered it, then patronized and humiliated his friends."},
  {"page":161,"id":"u6-a2-guardian-climax","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · guardian-climax","flag":[7.2,60.4],"answer":"The lodger was caught, the truth emerged, and Peter and Ginger took the money."},
  {"page":161,"id":"u6-a2-guardian-falling","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · guardian-falling","flag":[7.2,68.7],"answer":"Sam had to beg his friends to share the money."},
  {"page":161,"id":"u6-a2-guardian-resolution","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · guardian-resolution","flag":[7.2,76.7],"answer":"Peter decided to give some money back to Sam."},
  {"page":162,"id":"u6-a2-thunder-conflict","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · A Sound of Thunder · Conflict","flag":[7.2,15.7],"answer":"The safari would not guarantee that Eckels would come back alive from his trip to the past."},
  {"page":162,"id":"u6-a2-thunder-rising","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · thunder-rising","flag":[7.2,24.1],"answer":"They travelled to the past; the leaders instructed the hunters to stay on the path."},
  {"page":162,"id":"u6-a2-thunder-climax","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · thunder-climax","flag":[7.2,32.1],"answer":"Eckels stepped off the path and killed a butterfly."},
  {"page":162,"id":"u6-a2-thunder-falling","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · thunder-falling","flag":[7.2,40.5],"answer":"Travis, furious, threatened to leave Eckels in the past."},
  {"page":162,"id":"u6-a2-thunder-resolution","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 2 · thunder-resolution","flag":[7.2,48.6],"answer":"Back home, the United States had changed; Travis blamed Eckels and shot him."},
  {"page":165,"id":"u6-a4-1","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 4 · 1","flag":[7.2,26],"answer":"The elder sister took her swing."},
  {"page":165,"id":"u6-a4-2","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 4 · 2","flag":[7.2,32.3],"answer":"Her sister refused to get off the swing."},
  {"page":165,"id":"u6-a4-3","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 4 · 3","flag":[7.2,38.7],"answer":"“I” charged toward her sister, knocked her down and bit her."},
  {"page":165,"id":"u6-a4-4","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 4 · 4","flag":[7.2,44.9],"answer":"“I” bit her sister and got the swing back."},
  {"page":165,"id":"u6-a4-5","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 4 · 5","flag":[7.2,51.4],"answer":"The conflict continued."},
  {"page":167,"id":"u6-a5-t0-1","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 5 · 1","flag":[7.2,26.8],"answer":"The protagonist would like to leave the place they have entered."},
  {"page":167,"id":"u6-a5-t0-2","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 5 · 2","flag":[7.2,33.2],"answer":"Yes, both regretted that they had come to the place and wanted to leave."},
  {"page":171,"id":"u6-a7-climax","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · climax","flag":[41.9,68.6],"answer":"Mrs. Mallard declares that she is free."},
  {"page":171,"id":"u6-a7-falling","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · falling","flag":[57.8,73.8],"answer":"Mr. Mallard returns alive and shocks everyone."},
  {"page":171,"id":"u6-a7-rising","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · rising","flag":[21.1,74.1],"answer":"After briefly grieving, she goes alone to her room."},
  {"page":171,"id":"u6-a7-exposition","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · exposition","flag":[15.2,81.2],"answer":"Mrs. Mallard, who has heart trouble, is carefully told that her husband has died."},
  {"page":171,"id":"u6-a7-resolution","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · resolution","flag":[69.9,81.5],"answer":"The doctors say she died of joy."},
  {"page":172,"id":"u6-a7-t2-1","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · Task 2 · 1","flag":[7.2,17.5],"answer":"Mrs. Mallard had a heart trouble."},
  {"page":172,"id":"u6-a7-t2-2","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · Task 2 · 2","flag":[7.2,24],"answer":"Mrs. Mallard’s reaction, different from most women indicated that her relationship with her husband might be different from many others."},
  {"page":172,"id":"u6-a7-t2-3","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · Task 2 · 3","flag":[7.2,30.5],"answer":"Mrs. Mallard gradually thought about the news of her husband’s death and came to the conclusion that her husband’s death would lead to her freedom."},
  {"page":172,"id":"u6-a7-t2-4","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · Task 2 · 4","flag":[7.2,36.9],"answer":"Mrs. Mallard’s sister begged her to open the door which she refused. While her sister thought she was probably overwhelmed by grief, she was imagining what a beautiful life she had ahead for herself. This sharp contrast foreshadows what is coming next and also tells us more about the protagonist. The monologue by Mrs. Mallard declared her newly-gained freedom which was quite unexpected reaction for the audience…thus catching the readers’ attention. At the end, the indirect speech by the doctors indicated that people thought Mrs. Mallard loved Mr. Mallard so much she was overjoyed when she saw him back and caused her heart attack when the truth was that she was joyful for losing him and probably heartbroken shocked to see him back alive."},
  {"page":172,"id":"u6-a7-task2-5","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · Task 2 · 5","flag":[7.2,49.4],"answer":"Her husband’s friend Richard was “in the newspaper office”and was able to confirm it “with a second telegram” indicating probably her husband is also one of these people. “She did not hear the story as many women have heard the same, with a paralyzed inability to accept its significance”. This means that women were expected to act in a way society considered as proper. “She knew that she would weep again when she saw the kind, tender hands folded in death;“…“tender hands” might again indicate that this man has a higher social status --at least he was not making a living by using his hands. Also at the end, “It was Brently Mallard who entered, a little travel–stained, composedly carrying his gripsack and umbrella” probably he was travelling to do business."},
  {"page":172,"id":"u6-a7-task2-6","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 7 · Task 2 · 6","flag":[7.2,64],"answer":"“And yet she had loved him—sometimes.Often she had not. What did it matter! What could love, the unsolved mystery, count for in face of this possession of self–assertion which she suddenly recognized as the strongest impulse of her being!” also tells although she had affections sometimes toward her husband, she would prefer to have her own freedom."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t1-1","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 1 · 1","flag":[7.2,26.4],"answer":"It has been a tradition in Montreal, especially in the French part of Montreal, the animals are often given the names of saints. The horse was named after Good St. Joseph because Pierre thought he was “kind and gentle and faithful and a beautiful spirit.”"},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t1-2","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 1 · 2","flag":[7.2,32.4],"answer":"Within a year, Joseph knew the milk route as well as Pierre. He knows where to turn and where to stop. Pierre boasted that he didn’t need reins and a blind man could even handle the route with Joseph."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t1-3","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 1 · 3","flag":[7.2,38.7],"answer":"The cooks knew that Pierre could neither read nor write so instead of following the usual custom of leaving a note in an empty milk bottle if an additional quart of milk was needed they would give their orders orally."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t1-4","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 1 · 4","flag":[7.2,44.8],"answer":"Pierre was told that Joseph was dead in one early cold morning."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t1-5","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 1 · 5","flag":[7.2,51],"answer":"The ambulance doctor told Jacques that Pierre was blind."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t2-1","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 2 · 1","flag":[7.2,67.2],"answer":"Pierre loved the horse so much that he did not want to be separated from Joseph."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t2-2","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 2 · 2","flag":[7.2,73.5],"answer":"On the surface, Joseph was dead and another layer of meaning is that he was blind and he cannot see Joseph with his own eyes."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t2-3","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 2 · 3","flag":[7.2,79.5],"answer":"It indicates the close friendship between Pierre and Joseph. Only the horse knows his secret in the company and it keeps it for him."},
  {"page":176,"id":"u6-a8-t2-4","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 8 · Task 2 · 4","flag":[7.2,85.8],"answer":"Yes, the author offers details about the friendship between the driver and his horse(e.g. Pierre never touched his reins and highly praised Joseph all the time, he was panic-stricken when he knew he might part with Joseph, etc). Because the two have been such a matching couple in the milk delivery for so many years, no one was able to find that Pierre was blind for quite a while. The loss of Joseph was such a heavy blow to Pierre that he did not pay attention to the coming truck which killed him."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-1","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 1","flag":[7.2,20.4],"answer":"She was pressed down by a physical exhaustion that haunted her body and seemed to reach into her soul."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-2","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 2","flag":[7.2,24.6],"answer":"She could see in the open square before her house the tops of trees that were all aquiver with the new spring life."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-3","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 3","flag":[7.2,28.6],"answer":"She was young, with a fair, calm face, whose lines bespoke repression and even a certain strength."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-4","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 4","flag":[7.2,32.7],"answer":"But now there was a dull stare in her eyes, whose gaze was fixed away off yonder on one of those patches of blue sky. It was not a glance of reflection, but rather indicated a suspension of intelligent thought."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-5","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 5","flag":[7.2,38.8],"answer":"There was something coming to her and she was waiting for it, fearfully."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-6","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 6","flag":[7.2,42.9],"answer":"There would be no powerful will bending hers in that blind persistence with which men and women believe they have a right to impose a private will upon a fellow–creature."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a9-t0-7","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 9 · 7","flag":[7.2,49.1],"answer":"And yet she had loved him—sometimes. Often she had not."},
  {"page":180,"id":"u6-a10","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 10","flag":[7.2,59.3],"answer":"Reference: “her” refers to Mrs. Mallard.\n\nLexical chain: “her husband’s death,” “railroad disaster,” “killed,” “news,” “intelligence,” and “sad message.”\n\nConjunction: “and” in “and had hastened.”\n\nSubstitution: “the same” in “heard the same.”\n\nCohesive noun: “the storm of grief” refers back to her weeping.\n\nEllipsis: “She would have no one follow her.”"},
  {"page":181,"id":"u6-a11","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 11","flag":[7.2,32.2],"answer":"The air was clear and the sun reflected off beetle-sized cars inching along thousands of feet below us. One of them might have been a limousine, but it was too far away to tell."},
  {"page":181,"id":"u6-a12","label":"Unit 6 · Activity 12 · Correct order","flag":[7.2,56.6],"answer":"1 → 6 → 2 → 4 → 3 → 5"}
